#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The nightly check that no public repo drifts silently: the 11-13 audit, as code.

Why (11-13): an audit found 8 stale public repos, 7 stale or missing retros, and live code that
differed from the repo it came from. Nothing had failed; each had simply aged, and nobody runs an
audit by hand every night. This lists every public repo, says how it reaches the public (its kind),
and checks each kind the way it can drift:

  pushed     the public repo is the working clone. Drift: a clone with uncommitted changes (`dirty`), a
             checked-out branch with commits on no remote (`unpushed`), and a local branch that tracks
             the public default branch but carries commits not on it, so a plain push publishes them
             (`private-commits`).
  published  the public repo is a release of a private working repo through a PUBLISH whitelist
             (publish-public). Drift: what a publish would export now (the whitelisted files committed
             at the source's HEAD, agent working files dropped) differs from the public default
             branch (`unpublished`). The private repo being ahead of or behind the public one is not
             drift; only the export is compared.
  archived   read-only on GitHub; listed, not checked.
  declared   a public repo with no clone on purpose (config `declared`, with a reason).
  unknown    a public repo that is none of these: a FAILURE. Every public repo must have one way in.

Also, on whichever host each entry names: README auto-blocks that differ from a fresh render
(`readme-blocks check`, in every clone or source whose README has `<!-- auto:` markers), installed files
whose hash differs from their repo copy (`installed`), a retro older than a lane result that has sat
out of it for more than `max_days` (`retro`), and a backup command that no launchd job or crontab
line runs on a schedule (`backup`). A host that cannot be reached, a source that cannot be read, or a
GitHub call that fails is a problem too: the check fails closed.

Problems go to $RSTACK_STATE_DIR/asks-waiting/<host>-repos.tsv (the conductor's asks-waiting hook
prints it every turn) and each NEW one runs --alert once, the same way job-health reports. A run that
reported exits 0; only --dry (print, write nothing) exits 1 when it finds a problem.

    repo-freshness [--config FILE] [--alert CMD] [--push HOST] [--dry]

Config (default ~/.config/rstack/repo-freshness.json). Every entry may name a `host` (an ssh alias);
without one it is read on the machine running the check. Paths may start with ~ (that host's home).

    {"owners": ["example-user"],                          GitHub owners whose public repos are listed
     "roots": [{"path": "~/dev", "depth": 4},              where clones and PUBLISH files are found
               {"path": "~/dev", "depth": 4, "host": "other-mac"}],
     "exclude": ["~/dev/scratch/*"],                       clone or source paths (globs) never counted
     "declared": {"example-user/old-site": {"kind": "frozen", "why": "kept for links; no clone"}},
     "agent_files": "~/.config/git/agent-files.gitignore", dropped from a publish, as publish-public does
     "readme_blocks": "readme-blocks",                     the render/check command on each host
     "installed": [{"live": "~/.local/bin", "repo": "~/dev/tools/bin", "names": ["a-tool", "b-tool"]},
                   {"live": "~/.claude/hooks/gate.py", "repo": "~/dev/tools/hooks/gate.py"}],
     "retros": [{"lane": "demo", "retro": "~/dev/demo/RETRO.md",
                 "results": "~/.local/share/research-harness/lanes/demo/RESULTS.jsonl", "max_days": 7}],
     "backups": [{"name": "kb", "match": "backup-kb"}]}   a string some scheduled job's command contains
"""
